<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zakat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zakat is the obligatory almsgiving of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, one of the Five Pillars, levied as a proportion of certain kinds of wealth and distributed to the poor and other specified recipients. In Islamic states it also functioned as a tax. Each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> collected zakat on agricultural produce and livestock, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">kharaj</a>, remitting a portion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zakat.jpg" alt="Zakat" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SPQR10 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zakat_categories.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word zakat comes from the Arabic root z-k-w, meaning to purify. Muslims hold that it purifies income and wealth, as ablutions purify the body and prayer the soul. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> discusses charity in many verses, and zakat is found in the early Medinan suras and described as obligatory. Muslims believe that those who give it can expect reward from God in the afterlife. The practice is traditionally traced to Muhammad, and Schact suggested that the idea may have entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> word zakut, though some Islamic scholars disagree.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The caliph Abu Bakr was the first to institute a statutory zakat system, on the principle that it must be paid to the legitimate representative of Muhammad's authority. Tribes that refused to pay while remaining nominally Muslim were considered apostates, which led to the Ridda wars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uthman-ibn-affan">Uthman ibn Affan</a> decreed that only apparent wealth was taxable, which limited zakat mostly to agricultural land and produce. After Ali ibn Abu Talib his supporters refused to pay it to Muawiyah I.</p><p>State administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> was short-lived. During the reign of Umar bin Abdul Aziz (717–720 CE) it is reported that no one in Medina needed zakat, and after him it came to be seen more as an individual responsibility. Zakat was not collected from non-Muslims, who paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, and Muslims were also encouraged to give voluntary sadaqat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Zakat on wealth is customarily 2.5 per cent, or one fortieth, of disposable income after essential needs. It is payable on assets held for a lunar year above the nisab, a minimum value, and on agricultural goods, precious metals, minerals and livestock the rate varies between 2.5 and 20 per cent. Scholars differ on the nisab and on which kinds of wealth are zakatable.</p><p>Verse 9:60 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> is read as naming eight categories of recipient: the poor, the needy, the collectors, those whose hearts are to be won, those to be freed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, debtors, those in the cause of God, and stranded travellers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i school</a> requires equal division among the categories, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> permits giving to any or only one. Shia Muslims have traditionally given zakat to deputies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> rather than to the state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zakat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a layered fiscal structure: each of the caliphate's thirty or so emirates collected taxes according to Islamic principles, including <b>zakat</b> on produce and livestock, jizya on non-Muslim subjects and kharaj on agricultural land, remitting a portion to Sokoto while retaining the remainder for local administration and military expenditure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The practice of Islam rests on five pillars: the shahada; the five daily prayers, salat, performed facing Mecca; <b>zakat</b>, the giving of a share of one’s wealth to the poor; fasting from dawn to sunset during the month of Ramadan; and the hajj, the pilgrimage to Mecca, which…</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Townspeople paid the alms tax (<b>zakat</b>) rather than the poll tax, and they could make themselves heard: crowds in Baghdad rioted against the Turkish soldiers in 836, and took the side of Ahmad ibn Hanbal in the Mihna.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>It collected <b>zakat</b> from subject towns and tribes and destroyed shrines seen as idolatrous.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-diriyah">Emirate of Diriyah</a></li></ul></section>
